\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the PSL prototype showing the beam path (red lines) from the NPRO (top right) to the second amplifier (bottom right). The beam path up to the first amplifier includes optics for setting the polarisation of the beam, an EOM, a Faraday isolator and lenses for mode matching. After the first amplifier, the beam passes through a high power attenuator, mode matching lenses and a Faraday isolator. The first amplifier (bottom left) is monitored using a low power pick-off beam (centre right).\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.12}}
\newlabel{fig:o191520}{{3.3}{44}{Photograph of the PSL prototype showing the beam path (red lines) from the NPRO (top right) to the second amplifier (bottom right). The beam path up to the first amplifier includes optics for setting the polarisation of the beam, an EOM, a Faraday isolator and lenses for mode matching. After the first amplifier, the beam passes through a high power attenuator, mode matching lenses and a Faraday isolator. The first amplifier (bottom left) is monitored using a low power pick-off beam (centre right).\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the layout showing the beam path (red lines) from the second amplifier (bottom left) to the input of the PMC (centre right). This beam path includes a high power attenuator, an AOM, mode matching lenses for the PMC and a TFP. The PMC can be seen more clearly in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:o191531}. The path taken by light reflected from the PMC is shown. This light is used to lock the PMC. A low power beam for monitoring the second amplifier is obtained using the transmission through a steering mirror (centre top) for the alignment to the PMC.\relax }}{45}{figure.caption.13}}
\newlabel{fig:o191525}{{3.4}{45}{Photograph of the layout showing the beam path (red lines) from the second amplifier (bottom left) to the input of the PMC (centre right). This beam path includes a high power attenuator, an AOM, mode matching lenses for the PMC and a TFP. The PMC can be seen more clearly in Figure~\ref {fig:o191531}. The path taken by light reflected from the PMC is shown. This light is used to lock the PMC. A low power beam for monitoring the second amplifier is obtained using the transmission through a steering mirror (centre top) for the alignment to the PMC.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the PMC (centre) and the inner ISS photodiode box (top right). The beam (shown in red) from the second amplifier enters the PMC. The majority of this light is sent to the interferometer. A small fraction of the light in transmission of the PMC is used to stabilise the intensity and frequency of the laser. These beams are labelled as the ISS beam and the TTFSS beam. The ISS beam is directed into a light tight enclosure. This enclosure contains two photodiodes which are for measuring the intensity noise of the laser. One of these is an in-loop sensor; the other is an out-of-loop sensor. A QPD can be used to monitor the alignment of the ISS photodiodes. The reflected beam from the PMC is made up of the junk light in the beam from the second amplifier.\relax }}{46}{figure.caption.14}}
\newlabel{fig:o191531}{{3.5}{46}{Photograph of the PMC (centre) and the inner ISS photodiode box (top right). The beam (shown in red) from the second amplifier enters the PMC. The majority of this light is sent to the interferometer. A small fraction of the light in transmission of the PMC is used to stabilise the intensity and frequency of the laser. These beams are labelled as the ISS beam and the TTFSS beam. The ISS beam is directed into a light tight enclosure. This enclosure contains two photodiodes which are for measuring the intensity noise of the laser. One of these is an in-loop sensor; the other is an out-of-loop sensor. A QPD can be used to monitor the alignment of the ISS photodiodes. The reflected beam from the PMC is made up of the junk light in the beam from the second amplifier.\relax }{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the DBB with the beam path highlighted in red. Low power beams are used to measured properties of the laser produced by both amplifiers. The shutters control which amplifier will be characterised. The DBB contains motorised lenses and PZT mirrors so that remote mode matching and automatic alignment can be performed. The intensity noise of the beam can be measured with the RIN photodiode. The DBB locking photodiode is used to lock the cavity to the beam. This photodiode is also used during mode scans to measure the higher order content of the beam. QPDs separated by 90\si {\degree } of Gouy phase are used to measure the pointing noise of the incoming beam.\relax }}{47}{figure.caption.15}}
\newlabel{fig:o191542}{{3.6}{47}{Photograph of the DBB with the beam path highlighted in red. Low power beams are used to measured properties of the laser produced by both amplifiers. The shutters control which amplifier will be characterised. The DBB contains motorised lenses and PZT mirrors so that remote mode matching and automatic alignment can be performed. The intensity noise of the beam can be measured with the RIN photodiode. The DBB locking photodiode is used to lock the cavity to the beam. This photodiode is also used during mode scans to measure the higher order content of the beam. QPDs separated by 90\si {\degree } of Gouy phase are used to measure the pointing noise of the incoming beam.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
