\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces The top panel shows the power that was incident on the PMC and the power emerging from the PMC over a 55 hour trail run. The light produced by the amplifiers did not change significantly for the duration of this measurement, however the transmitted light through the PMC dropped from 111\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt } to, at its lowest, 107\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\watt }. This is likely due to the environmental conditions in the lab changing. The bottom panel shows the humidity of the lab. Changes in the humidity, which can be caused by a temperature change, coincide with changes in the power emerging from the PMC. This indicates that the alignment or mode matching of the laser and the PMC may be dependent on temperature due to e.g.\nobreakspace  {}the thermal expansion of optical mounts.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.16}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterpowerplot}{{3.7}{54}{The top panel shows the power that was incident on the PMC and the power emerging from the PMC over a 55 hour trail run. The light produced by the amplifiers did not change significantly for the duration of this measurement, however the transmitted light through the PMC dropped from 111\,\si {\watt } to, at its lowest, 107\,\si {\watt }. This is likely due to the environmental conditions in the lab changing. The bottom panel shows the humidity of the lab. Changes in the humidity, which can be caused by a temperature change, coincide with changes in the power emerging from the PMC. This indicates that the alignment or mode matching of the laser and the PMC may be dependent on temperature due to e.g.~the thermal expansion of optical mounts.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Cartoons of water cooling layout for the prototype laser. Panel (a): The laser will be run with the amplifier heads cooled in series with each other to reduce pointing noise. Panel (b): To perform the amplifier head temperature experiment, the water cooling was arranged so that the amplifiers temperature could be independently controlled.\relax }}{55}{figure.caption.17}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapterwatercoolingschematic}{{3.8}{55}{Cartoons of water cooling layout for the prototype laser. Panel (a): The laser will be run with the amplifier heads cooled in series with each other to reduce pointing noise. Panel (b): To perform the amplifier head temperature experiment, the water cooling was arranged so that the amplifiers temperature could be independently controlled.\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: The higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature increased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }}{56}{figure.caption.18}}
\newlabel{fig:ligo_laser_chapter_cooling_pwoer_hom}{{3.9}{56}{The top panel: as the second amplifier head's temperature increased, the power produced by it decreased. The bottom panel: The higher order mode content increased as the temperature of the amplifier increased. This means that the power in the fundamental mode decreased as the temperature increased due to two effects: less overall power and worse beam quality.\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Mode scans were performed with the DBB for each amplifier head temperature. The DBB's auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim 0.3$ FSR; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. There is some power in the fourth order mode. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }}{57}{figure.caption.19}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchaptermodescans}{{3.10}{57}{Mode scans were performed with the DBB for each amplifier head temperature. The DBB's auto-alignment system minimised the power in the first order mode. The second order mode for this cavity is located at $\sim 0.3$ FSR; its exact location drifts due to thermal expansion of the cavity. There is some power in the fourth order mode. As the temperature of the amplifier head changes, the second order mode content of the beam changes at the percent level. This second order mode height is shown in the inset. The second order mode changes due to the beam's astigmatism changing.\relax }{figure.caption.19}{}}
