\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces A sketch of how the AOM transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo has also been shown. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An IC (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the AOM's effect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are found in\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {ISS_inner_loop_rack} and\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {ISS_inner_loop_photodiode}.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.23}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}{{3.14}{65}{A sketch of how the AOM transfer function was measured. The components of the intensity stabilisation servo has also been shown. The PMC was locked and a signal analyser was used to perform a swept sine measurement. An IC (AD734) that performs a square root operation to an input signal was used to linearise the AOM's effect on beam power to an input signal. The transfer function was measured between the input to the lineariser and the photodiode used for the intensity stabilisation. The servo electronics are found in~\cite {ISS_inner_loop_rack} and~\cite {ISS_inner_loop_photodiode}.\relax }{figure.caption.23}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Using the layout shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the AOM to the light power change on the ISS photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where the open-loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\si {\kilo \hertz }$). There is a some phase lag between 10-100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz; this is where the unity gain point of the servo will be and so this needs to be accounted for when determining if the loop will be stable. The corner frequency of the PMC is around $\sim 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {MHz}$, so this did not affect the measurement.\relax }}{66}{figure.caption.24}}
\newlabel{fig:ligolaserchapteraomtransferfunction}{{3.15}{66}{Using the layout shown in Figure~\ref {fig:ligolaserchapteraomtfmeasurement}, the transfer function between the input to the linearisation stage before the AOM to the light power change on the ISS photodiode was measured. The response was normalised by dividing by the DC power on the photodiode. The transfer function is flat over the region where the open-loop gain needs to be greater than one ($\sim 10\,\si {\kilo \hertz }$). There is a some phase lag between 10-100\,kHz; this is where the unity gain point of the servo will be and so this needs to be accounted for when determining if the loop will be stable. The corner frequency of the PMC is around $\sim 1\,\rm {MHz}$, so this did not affect the measurement.\relax }{figure.caption.24}{}}
