\citation{Hooge1981}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces The dark noise of the FD10D increases as the reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $i_c/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the data. See appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ and above 0.9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. The values for $\beta $ are typical for `$1/f$' noise\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {Hooge1981}. The dark noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz for each reverse bias is shown in Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot} alongside the results for the other photodiodes that were tested.\relax }}{193}{figure.caption.78}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterfd10ddarknoise}{{7.5}{193}{The dark noise of the FD10D increases as the reverse bias is increased. Above 0.6\,V, the dark noise of the FD10D would limit the noise of a 10\,mA current above 10\,Hz. As the noise spectral densities have no knees, the function $i_c/f^{\beta }$ can be fitted to the data. See appendix~\ref {sec:pd_appendix_bias_dark_noise} for the dark noise spectra of other photodiodes. Below 0.9\,V reverse bias, the spectra fall as $f^{-0.45}$ and above 0.9\,V bias the spectra fall as $f^{-0.4}$. The values for $\beta $ are typical for `$1/f$' noise~\cite {Hooge1981}. The dark noise at 10\,Hz for each reverse bias is shown in Figure~\ref {fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot} alongside the results for the other photodiodes that were tested.\relax }{figure.caption.78}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode was measured. Photodiodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm photodiodes. See Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:pd_chapter_photodiodes_tested} for information about the size and cut-off wavelength for each photodiode. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz for each photodiode. The dashed line indicates the level of shot noise for a 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mA current. The data used to create these plots are shown in Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:dark_noise_rev_bias_plots}.\relax }}{194}{figure.caption.79}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterdarkcurrentmegaplot}{{7.6}{194}{Left panel: the dark current for each photodiode was measured. Photodiodes of a similar cut-off wavelength share the same marker shape. The 1\,mm photodiodes have larger markers than the 0.5\,mm photodiodes. See Table~\ref {tab:pd_chapter_photodiodes_tested} for information about the size and cut-off wavelength for each photodiode. Right panel: the dark noise at 10\,Hz for each photodiode. The dashed line indicates the level of shot noise for a 10\,mA current. The data used to create these plots are shown in Appendix~\ref {app:dark_noise_rev_bias_plots}.\relax }{figure.caption.79}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces The difference in dark noise between two nominally identical photodiodes (g12182-010k) under the same operating conditions (room temperature at 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V bias). Photodiode 1 performs nearly 100 times better than photodiode 2. For the duration of this experiment, the photodiodes were handled with suitable ESD precautions. This difference in noise may be explained by differing numbers of defects or by ESD damage prior to purchase.\relax }}{195}{figure.caption.80}}
\newlabel{fig:pdchapterg12182-010knoisecompare}{{7.7}{195}{The difference in dark noise between two nominally identical photodiodes (g12182-010k) under the same operating conditions (room temperature at 1\,V bias). Photodiode 1 performs nearly 100 times better than photodiode 2. For the duration of this experiment, the photodiodes were handled with suitable ESD precautions. This difference in noise may be explained by differing numbers of defects or by ESD damage prior to purchase.\relax }{figure.caption.80}{}}
