\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces (a) Sketch of a Mach-Zehnder interferometer. The interference at BS2 depends on the difference in the phase of the two beams after BS1. (b) Sketch of an interferometer (IFO) with a balanced homodyne detection scheme. The IFO could be, for instance, a Michelson interferometer. The IFO adds a phase modulation to the signal beam, and this results in an intensity modulation in the combined beams after BS2. In essence, (b) is similar to (a).\relax }}{217}{figure.caption.90}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchaptermzsketch}{{8.1}{217}{(a) Sketch of a Mach-Zehnder interferometer. The interference at BS2 depends on the difference in the phase of the two beams after BS1. (b) Sketch of an interferometer (IFO) with a balanced homodyne detection scheme. The IFO could be, for instance, a Michelson interferometer. The IFO adds a phase modulation to the signal beam, and this results in an intensity modulation in the combined beams after BS2. In essence, (b) is similar to (a).\relax }{figure.caption.90}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.1.1}Apparatus for the In-Air Mach-Zehnder.}{217}{subsection.8.1.1}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Photograph of the Mach-Zehnder base plate. The polarising beam splitters were part of an earlier design; they were removed when the measurements with this apparatus were made. \relax }}{218}{figure.caption.91}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairmz}{{8.2}{218}{Photograph of the Mach-Zehnder base plate. The polarising beam splitters were part of an earlier design; they were removed when the measurements with this apparatus were made. \relax }{figure.caption.91}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Sketch of the optical layout. The laser was generated by an NPRO. The power of the laser was monitored using a photodiode immediately after the laser. The beam's power and polarisation were controlled with a half wave plate and polarising beam splitter (PBS). The Mach-Zehnder was mounted on a base plate, and it was acoustically shielded by a plastic box and rubber feet. The splitting ratio of the BS2 was measured to be 50:50. An ND filter was used to decrease the power in one of the paths within the Mach-Zehnder. \relax }}{219}{figure.caption.92}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairsketch}{{8.3}{219}{Sketch of the optical layout. The laser was generated by an NPRO. The power of the laser was monitored using a photodiode immediately after the laser. The beam's power and polarisation were controlled with a half wave plate and polarising beam splitter (PBS). The Mach-Zehnder was mounted on a base plate, and it was acoustically shielded by a plastic box and rubber feet. The splitting ratio of the BS2 was measured to be 50:50. An ND filter was used to decrease the power in one of the paths within the Mach-Zehnder. \relax }{figure.caption.92}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces The transfer function of the electronics for sending, receiving and whitening the photodiode signal was modelled and measured. This gave the desired level of SNR to make shot noise limited measurements above $\sim 200\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\rm  {Hz}$.\relax }}{222}{figure.caption.93}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinairsendrecievefilter}{{8.4}{222}{The transfer function of the electronics for sending, receiving and whitening the photodiode signal was modelled and measured. This gave the desired level of SNR to make shot noise limited measurements above $\sim 200\,\rm {Hz}$.\relax }{figure.caption.93}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces Circuit diagram for the electronics used to keep the in-air Mach-Zehnder locked to the middle of a fringe. The inputs PD\_A and PD\_B connect to two identical transimpedance amplifiers (see Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}). The first stage subtracts the signals from the photodiodes and provides a 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz roll-off. The second stage allowed for a signal to be applied to the PZT. The final stage boosted the signal to around 100V. The final resistor was used to prevent the OPA454 oscillating when driving the PZT, as the PZT is a capacitive load.\relax }}{223}{figure.caption.94}}
\newlabel{fig:mzinairservocircuit}{{8.5}{223}{Circuit diagram for the electronics used to keep the in-air Mach-Zehnder locked to the middle of a fringe. The inputs PD\_A and PD\_B connect to two identical transimpedance amplifiers (see Appendix~\ref {sec:circuits_appendix_variable_bias_ad797}). The first stage subtracts the signals from the photodiodes and provides a 1\,Hz roll-off. The second stage allowed for a signal to be applied to the PZT. The final stage boosted the signal to around 100V. The final resistor was used to prevent the OPA454 oscillating when driving the PZT, as the PZT is a capacitive load.\relax }{figure.caption.94}{}}
