\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.1}{\ignorespaces This MEDM interface is used by operators of the interferometer to control and monitor the status of the PMC. Several inputs and outputs can be monitored simultaneously, and locking the PMC is reduced to simply pressing a button (\texttt  {LOCK}). Most aspects of the locking servo can be adjusted with CDS, e.g. the \texttt  {gain} slider adjusts the gain of the servo, thus operators have a large amount of freedom when they are trying to optimise the PMC's locking capabilities.\relax }}{276}{figure.caption.129}}
\newlabel{fig:medmscreen}{{C.1}{276}{This MEDM interface is used by operators of the interferometer to control and monitor the status of the PMC. Several inputs and outputs can be monitored simultaneously, and locking the PMC is reduced to simply pressing a button (\texttt {LOCK}). Most aspects of the locking servo can be adjusted with CDS, e.g. the \texttt {gain} slider adjusts the gain of the servo, thus operators have a large amount of freedom when they are trying to optimise the PMC's locking capabilities.\relax }{figure.caption.129}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {C.2}{\ignorespaces Noise floor of the digitised input signals recorded by the Glasgow CDS. This was converted into a voltage using $2^{15}$ counts per 10 Volts. The ripples above 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz arise due to the 3rd order low pass filter at 9\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}kHz. Below $\sim 10$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the input noise rises as $1/f$.\relax }}{278}{figure.caption.130}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchaptercdsnoise}{{C.2}{278}{Noise floor of the digitised input signals recorded by the Glasgow CDS. This was converted into a voltage using $2^{15}$ counts per 10 Volts. The ripples above 1\,kHz arise due to the 3rd order low pass filter at 9\,kHz. Below $\sim 10$\,Hz, the input noise rises as $1/f$.\relax }{figure.caption.130}{}}
